WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:07.629
DRAGONN is a randomized hashing algorithm for
gradient sparsification in data-parallel distributed training,

00:00:07.709 --> 00:00:09.831
from Rice University and ThirdAI.

00:00:09.911 --> 00:00:11.433
In distributed training,

00:00:11.513 --> 00:00:16.106
synchronizing gradients across GPUs is the main
efficiency bottleneck,

00:00:16.186 --> 00:00:19.777
and gradient sparsification methods were meant to help,

00:00:19.857 --> 00:00:24.115
but their own compression overhead has become the
new bottleneck.

00:00:24.195 --> 00:00:30.790
DRAGONN replaces the exact parallel-prefix-sum
operations used by prior methods with direct hashing,

00:00:30.870 --> 00:00:36.597
cutting compression time by up to seventy percent
and delivering up to three-and-a-half

00:00:36.677 --> 00:00:38.800
times faster training throughput.

00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.387
Data-parallel distributed training is the standard
way to scale deep learning across many GPUs,

00:00:45.467 --> 00:00:49.689
but synchronizing gradients between workers is the
dominant cost.

00:00:49.769 --> 00:00:55.579
Gradient sparsification promised relief by
transmitting only a small subset of gradients,

00:00:55.659 --> 00:01:01.403
yet in practice the time spent compressing the
gradients grew so large that it cancelled

00:01:01.483 --> 00:01:03.388
out the communication savings.

00:01:03.468 --> 00:01:07.822
The paper shows that once a tensor exceeds about
sixteen megabytes,

00:01:07.902 --> 00:01:12.786
this compression overhead becomes the single
largest efficiency bottleneck,

00:01:12.866 --> 00:01:18.080
and for small tensors sparsification can even be
slower than sending everything.

00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:22.557
The leading sparsification method, Deep Gradient
Compression,

00:01:22.637 --> 00:01:28.173
selects gradients above an estimated threshold,
which is an approximate operation.

00:01:28.253 --> 00:01:34.473
But to place those gradients into memory without
conflicts it relies on parallel prefix sum,

00:01:34.553 --> 00:01:38.171
an exact algorithm that builds a balanced binary tree,

00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:40.705
runs in logarithmic sequential steps,

00:01:40.785 --> 00:01:45.841
and touches memory about seven times more than the
theoretical lower bound.

00:01:45.921 --> 00:01:53.510
The authors call this an exact-approximate
mismatch: since only an approximate set of top gradients is required,

00:01:53.590 --> 00:01:58.030
using an expensive exact algorithm to place them is
wasted effort.

00:01:58.410 --> 00:02:01.922
The paper makes three contributions.

00:02:02.002 --> 00:02:05.513
First, it proposes DRAGONN,

00:02:05.593 --> 00:02:09.105
a hashing-based sparsification algorithm that
slashes compression overhead while keeping

00:02:09.185 --> 00:02:12.697
the same per-iteration convergence,

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:16.288
backed by theoretical bounds on both compression
error and generalization error.

00:02:16.368 --> 00:02:19.880
Second,

00:02:19.960 --> 00:02:23.472
it adds two system-level optimizations: an
efficiency-aware tensor selection that only

00:02:23.552 --> 00:02:27.063
compresses tensors where it actually pays off,

00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:30.655
and a sparse decoding scheme that keeps decode cost
from growing with the number of GPUs.

00:02:30.735 --> 00:02:34.247
Third,

00:02:34.327 --> 00:02:37.838
it evaluates the method broadly across vision and
recommendation models and demonstrates

00:02:37.918 --> 00:02:41.430
substantial end-to-end training speedups.

00:02:41.810 --> 00:02:47.571
DRAGONN works by pre-allocating a small memory
buffer sized to the compression ratio.

00:02:47.651 --> 00:02:51.695
For each gradient whose absolute value clears the
threshold,

00:02:51.775 --> 00:02:56.093
it hashes the gradient's index to a slot and writes
it directly,

00:02:56.173 --> 00:02:59.529
rather than scanning to compute nonzero positions.

00:02:59.609 --> 00:03:04.202
If two indices collide, the later one simply
overwrites the earlier,

00:03:04.282 --> 00:03:06.745
and any slot left empty maps to zero.

00:03:06.825 --> 00:03:10.456
Because writing memory is an atomic operation on GPUs,

00:03:10.536 --> 00:03:16.023
many threads can hash and write at the same time
with no dependency between them,

00:03:16.103 --> 00:03:21.040
so the whole compression needs only d comparisons
plus l hash operations,

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:23.788
essentially the theoretical lower bound.

00:03:23.868 --> 00:03:25.025
To deploy it well,

00:03:25.105 --> 00:03:31.142
DRAGONN adds two tricks: it only compresses a
tensor when the compression time is smaller

00:03:31.222 --> 00:03:33.685
than the communication it would save,

00:03:33.765 --> 00:03:40.076
and it batches all the compressed tensors received
from other workers into one sparse decode,

00:03:40.156 --> 00:03:45.230
so decoding cost stays nearly constant no matter
how many GPUs participate.

00:03:45.610 --> 00:03:52.499
The experiments span four models covering both
vision and recommendation-style workloads:

00:03:52.579 --> 00:03:54.770
ResNet50 trained on ImageNet,

00:03:54.850 --> 00:04:01.426
Vision Transformer and MLP-Mixer fine-tuned on
Cifar10 from ImageNet-21k pretraining,

00:04:01.506 --> 00:04:07.221
and an extreme multi-label classification model on
the Wiki10-31K dataset.

00:04:07.301 --> 00:04:15.287
The testbed is sixteen Nvidia V100 GPUs across two
machines connected by a twenty-five gigabit network,

00:04:15.367 --> 00:04:18.262
running PyTorch with Horovod and NCCL,

00:04:18.342 --> 00:04:25.780
and using memory-momentum error feedback to
preserve accuracy across all sparsification methods.

00:04:26.160 --> 00:04:32.174
The headline result is that DRAGONN cuts
compression time by up to seventy percent compared

00:04:32.254 --> 00:04:35.120
to the best existing sparsification methods.

00:04:35.200 --> 00:04:37.731
Reaching the same level of convergence,

00:04:37.811 --> 00:04:44.762
it delivers up to three-and-a-half times higher
total training throughput than Deep Gradient Compression,

00:04:44.842 --> 00:04:49.383
and up to nearly thirty-six times over full
gradient synchronization,

00:04:49.463 --> 00:04:53.467
all while matching the test accuracy of full
synchronization.

00:04:53.547 --> 00:04:57.820
Importantly, its advantage over DGC grows as more
GPUs are added,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.030
which signals strong scalability,

00:05:00.110 --> 00:05:04.650
and the authors expect the gains to widen further
on faster networks.

00:05:05.030 --> 00:05:09.768
An ablation study separates the contributions of
the three components.

00:05:09.848 --> 00:05:14.518
The hashing-based compressor by itself provides the
base improvement;

00:05:14.598 --> 00:05:19.199
layering on efficiency-aware tensor selection
increases the speedup,

00:05:19.279 --> 00:05:22.985
for example to about one-point-nine times on one model,

00:05:23.065 --> 00:05:27.872
and adding sparse decoding pushes it as high as
four-point-three times.

00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:32.897
Notably, the benefit of sparse decoding can exceed
the number of workers,

00:05:32.977 --> 00:05:39.230
because it eliminates the decode cost that
otherwise grows linearly with the number of GPUs.

00:05:39.310 --> 00:05:45.081
Micro-benchmarks confirm DRAGONN has the lowest
encoding time across all tensor sizes

00:05:45.161 --> 00:05:49.280
and that its decode time stays nearly flat as GPUs
are added.

00:05:49.660 --> 00:05:57.455
The key numbers are: up to a seventy percent
reduction in compression time versus state-of-the-art sparsification;

00:05:57.535 --> 00:06:03.395
up to three-and-a-half times faster total training
than DGC and up to thirty-six times

00:06:03.475 --> 00:06:07.125
faster than full synchronization at equal convergence;

00:06:07.205 --> 00:06:11.546
per-model speedups over DGC of about one-point-four
on ResNet50,

00:06:11.626 --> 00:06:15.069
two-point-two on ViT, one-point-seven on MLP-Mixer,

00:06:15.149 --> 00:06:18.315
and three-point-five on extreme classification;

00:06:18.395 --> 00:06:24.670
and a decoding overhead reduced from growing
linearly with the GPU count to nearly constant.

00:06:25.050 --> 00:06:32.604
The lasting lesson is simple: since gradient
sparsification only ever needs an approximate set of top gradients,

00:06:32.684 --> 00:06:36.080
it should not pay for an exact selection algorithm.

00:06:36.160 --> 00:06:40.715
By swapping exact parallel prefix sum for direct
randomized hashing,

00:06:40.795 --> 00:06:42.556
DRAGONN turns a sequential,

00:06:42.636 --> 00:06:47.327
dependency-heavy compression step into an
embarrassingly parallel one,

00:06:47.407 --> 00:06:52.848
removes the overhead that had been cancelling out
sparsification's benefits, and,

00:06:52.928 --> 00:06:56.324
together with tensor selection and sparse decoding,

00:06:56.404 --> 00:07:02.050
makes gradient sparsification finally pay off at
scale without sacrificing accuracy.
